{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows end-user-observable behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to draft a requirement for it and take it through review, agreement, and commit before implementing that behavior.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict.** The test adequately checks the subject. I found no gap.\n\n**Obligation.** `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change.uncovered` has two parts:\n- When implementation shows behavior no requirement covers, the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to draft a requirement for it.\n- The skill must then take that requirement through review, agreement, and commit before the behavior is implemented.\n\n**What the skill says.** In `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md#section[Habeas%20requirements]/section[Workflow]`:\n- The closing paragraph says \"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2.\"\n- Step 2 is \"Draft.\"\n- Step 3 is Review.\n- Step 4 is Present, with a **Gate** that allows no implementation code and no commit until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\n- Step 5 is Commit, \"before any code.\"\n- Step 6 is Implement and test.\n\n**Test mapping.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_drafts_a_requirement_for_uncovered_behavior]` makes two assertions:\n- It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", [\"Behavior no requirement covers goes back to step 2\"])`. That runs `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` on the collapsed bundled skill text. Removing or rewording the routing sentence fails the test.\n- It asserts `step(\"habeas-requirements\", 2).starts_with(\"Draft.\")`. `step` uses `workflow_steps`, which parses the numbered Workflow list. This fails if the step the behavior is routed to stops being the drafting step, or if the numbering changes.\n\nTogether these check the obligation's distinctive part: uncovered behavior is routed back to the drafting step.\n\n**Review, agreement, and commit before implementing.** This test does not assert the ordering of steps 3 to 6. That ordering is the same linear workflow that \"goes back to step 2\" re-enters. Other tests in the same file cover it, though I did not read them. They are named in the evidence rationale and in search snippets, and I did not check their `{test}` marks:\n- the test for the commit-before-code requirement\n- the test for the review and present steps\n- the test for `agreed_text`\n\nThese sibling requirements carry that behavior, so I treated it as delegated. A change that broke the 3→5→6 ordering would fail those tests. A change that dropped the routing sentence or changed step 2 would fail this one. I found no plausible way to break the subject's behavior that all of these tests would accept.\n\n**Other points.**\n- The check is a text-presence check against the skill, which suits a requirement about what skill text must direct.\n- The phrase is not tied to the \"implementation shows\" condition. The sentence is distinctive, so I don't count this as a gap.\n- No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply, and none are needed.",
